      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2016 World Obesity.",
        "AbstractText": "We report, for the first time, a case of an accumulation of glycogen in the liver causing elevation of liver enzymes in a 15-year-old morbidly obese adolescent male with well-controlled type 2 diabetes. Notably, the patient did not have poorly controlled type 1 diabetes and did not require insulin. After normalization of the A1c with metformin, elevated liver enzymes returned to normal."
      },
